===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|joint}} {{lb|en|anatomy}} The [[joint]] between the [[upper arm]] and the [[forearm]].
#* {{quote-web|en|year=1627|author={{w|Michael Drayton}}|title=The Moone-Calfe|work=English Poetry 1579-1830: Spenser and the Tradition|url=http://spenserians.cath.vt.edu/TextRecord.php?action=GET&textsid=33291|passage=Up to the '''elbowes''' naked were there Armes.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author={{w|Robert W. Chambers}}|title=The Younger Set|chapter=VIII|passage='''Elbows''' almost touching they leaned at ease, idly reading the almost obliterated lines engraved there. &para; {{nowrap|"I never}} understood it," she observed, lightly scornful. "What occult meaning has a sun-dial for the spooney? ''I'm'' sure I don't want to read riddles in a strange gentleman's optics."|year=1907|url=http://gutenberg.org/ebooks/14852}}
#: {{syn|en|elbow joint}}
# {{lb|en|by extension}} Any turn or bend like that of the elbow, in a wall, building, coastline, etc.; an angular or jointed part of any structure, such as the raised arm of a chair or sofa, or a short pipe fitting, turning at an angle or bent.
#: {{ux|en|the sides of windows, where the jamb makes an '''elbow''' with the window back}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1869|author=Richard Doddridge Blackmore|title=Lorna Doone: A Romance of Exmoor
|passage=The water runs down with a strong, sharp stickle, and then has a sudden '''elbow''' in it, where the small brook trickles in; and on that side the bank is steep, four or it may be five feet high, overhanging loamily; {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|US|dated|early 20th-century|_|slang}} A [[detective]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1924|author={{w|Dashiell Hammett}}|title=Zigzags of Treachery|passage="An '''elbow''', huh?" putting all the contempt he could in his voice; and somehow any synonym for detective seems able to hold a lot of contempt.}}
# {{lb|en|basketball}} Part of a [[basketball court]] located at the intersection of the [[free-throw line]] and the [[free-throw lane]].<ref>Newell, Pete; Nater, Swen (2008). ''[http://books.google.com/books?id=HMrmetcycyYC&lpg=PA26&dq=free%20throw%20line%20elbow&pg=PA26#v=onepage&q&f=false Pete Newell's Playing Big]''. Human Kinetics. p.26: [[Special:BookSources/9780736068093|ISBN 9780736068093]]. Retrieved April 11, 2013.</ref>
# A [[hit#Noun|hit]] with the elbow.
#* {{quote-web|en|author=Phil McNulty|authorlink=Phil McNulty|title=[[w:Tunisia national football team|Tunisia]] 1 – 2 [[w:England national football team|England]]|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20190421215852/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/44434276|archivedate=21 April 2019|work={{w|BBC Sport}}|date=18 June 2018|passage=England ran Tunisia ragged in that spell but were punished for missing a host of chances when {{w|Ferjani Sassi}} equalised from the penalty spot against the run of play after {{w|Kyle Walker}} was penalised for an '''elbow''' on {{w|Fakhreddine Ben Youssef}}.}}
# {{lb|en|knots}} Two nearby [[crossing]]s of a [[rope]].

