===Verb===
{{en-verb}} {{tlb|en|transitive}}

# To [[push]] with the elbow or elbows; to forge ahead using the elbows to assist.
#: {{ux|en|He '''elbowed''' his way through the crowd.}}
#* {{RQ:Doyle Land of Mist|passage=Through the crush Malone and Roxton '''elbowed''' their way until they reached Challenger's side, and partly by judicious propulsion, partly by artful persuasion, they got him, still bellowing his grievances, out of the building.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2012|author=Andrew Martin|title=Underground Overground: A passenger's history of the Tube|publisher=Profile Books, {{ISBN|978-1846684777}}|page=277|text=On the [[DLR]], or on the driverless Line 14 on the Paris Metro, I always try to sit at the front. (It's usually just a matter of '''elbowing''' aside some ten-year-old boys; I can then get on with pretending to drive the train.)}}
# To [[strike#Verb|strike]] with the elbow.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=George Lamming|authorlink=George Lamming|title={{w|In the Castle of My Skin}}|publisher=[[w:McGraw-Hill Education|McGraw Hill Books Company, Inc.]]|year=1953|year_published=1954|page=166|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/incastleofmyskin00lamm/page/166/mode/1up|passage=Trumper '''elbowed''' me in the ribs and made a sign with his head. He seemed irritated now by our delay.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Marjorie Darke|title=A Question of Courage|location=Harmondsworth, Middlesex|publisher=Kestrel Books|year=1975|page=108|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/questionofcourag0000dark_s6b8/page/108/mode/1up|isbn=0-722-65903-2|passage=She looked round for Vera, but could not see her, and in the process of wriggling through the heaving crowd was '''elbowed''' in the eye. The blow acted like a spur, putting one thought in her head . . . to escape.}}
#* {{RQ:Guardian Online|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20210210205234/https://www.theguardian.com/lifeandstyle/2001/nov/24/familyandrelationships|author=Mil Millington|authorlink=Mil Millington|title=Things my girlfriend and I argue about|date=24 November 2001|passage=Anyway, Margret is already asleep. I put my head on the pillow and am just about to inch warmth-seekingly closer to her when she suddenly '''elbows''' me full in the face with stupefying force. My howl of pain wakes her and she glances over her shoulder at her elbow, still embedded in my skull.}}
#* {{RQ:NYT|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20221220013849/https://www.nytimes.com/2022/05/04/sports/basketball/steph-curry-warriors-grizzlies-playoffs.html|author=Tania Ganguli|title=Against Grizzlies, Golden State Warriors Feel Range of Emotions|date=4 May 2022|passage=Green also left the game in the first quarter after {{w|Xavier Tillman}} inadvertently '''elbowed''' him in the face. Hearing boos from the crowd, [[w:Draymond Green|&#91;Draymond&#93; Green]] raised his middle fingers toward the fans as he left the court to get stitches above his right eye.}}
# To [[nudge]], [[jostle]] or [[push]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1993|title=A Fatal Thaw|author=Dana Stabenow |page=105 |ISBN= 0425135772|passage=Suddenly and with all her heart Kate longed to be home, back at the homestead, to participate in the rambunctious toss and jostle as breakup '''elbowed''' its way into the Park. }}
# {{lb|en|with "out" or "aside"}} To make someone quit or lose their job so that someone else can get it.

